{{also|Dido|ɗiɗo}}
==English==
===Etymology 1===
Origin unknown. The "trick" sense might come from the trick of [[Dido]], queen of Carthage, who, having bought as much land as a hide would cover, is said to have cut it into thin strips long enough to enclose a spot for a citadel.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|didoes}}

# {{context|slang|regional|lang=en}} A [[fuss]], a [[row]].
#*'''1974''', {{w|GB Edwards}}, ''The Book of Ebenezer Le Page'', New York 2007, p. 30:
#*:I remember Raymond telling me years later how when he lived at home, if his mother heard he had been seen as much as talking to a girl, she would kick up a '''dido'''.
# A [[shrewd]] [[trick]]; an [[antic]]; a [[caper]].
#: ''to cut a '''dido'''''
#*'''1838''', Joseph Clay Neal, ''Charcoal Sketches; Or, Scenes in a Metropolis'', [http://books.google.ru/books?id=Y8Tgvsg3vq4C&dq=cut%20%22didoes%22&pg=PA201#v=onepage&q&f=false p. 201]
#*:Young people," interposed a passing official, " if you keep a '''cutting didoes''', I must talk to you both like a [[dutch uncle|Dutch uncle]].

